Package: bsfa
Type: Package
Title: Bayesian Stochastic Frontier Analysis
Version: 0.2.0.9000
Authors@R: person(given = c("Franz", "X."),
                  family = "Mohr",
                  email = "franz.x.mohr@outlook.com",
                  role = c("aut", "cre"),
                  comment = c(ORCID = "0009-0003-8890-7781"))
Description: Bayesian inference for stochastic frontier models by Markov chain
    Monte Carlo. Gibbs samplers with data augmentation are provided for
    composed-error frontier models in the tradition of van den Broeck, Koop,
    Osiewalski and Steel (1994), covering exponential, half-normal and
    truncated normal inefficiency, production and cost frontiers, and
    cross-sectional, time-invariant panel and four-component panel
    specifications. The scale of an inefficiency term may depend on
    covariates, as in Caudill, Ford and Gropper (1995), and the
    pre-truncation mean of a truncated normal one may too, as in Battese and
    Coelli (1995); those coefficients have no conjugate full conditional and
    are drawn by Metropolis steps within the sweep. A model is built up step
    by step, by creating a model object and then adding priors, initial
    values and a seed to it before the posterior is drawn. Posterior
    distributions of unit-specific efficiency scores are obtained directly
    from the augmented draws rather than from point predictors.
